WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.360
SILO:
Hey guys, for those of you who want the summary,

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:07.120
we’re launching an investing app centered
around treasuries and bonds called Silo.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.330
We’ve been working on this app for past
20 months and we’re really excited for you

00:00:15.641 --> 00:00:18.851
letting people off the waitlist by the end
of the year.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:22.811
If you would like to be one of the first people
to get access to Silo, please consider joining

00:00:22.811 --> 00:00:24.401
our waitlist below.

00:00:24.401 --> 00:00:28.571
For those of you who wanna know more, let
me explain.

00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:36.261
WHAT IS SILO?:
Silo is an investing platform that is looking

00:00:36.261 --> 00:00:40.091
bring the 21st century to the underappreciated
market of bonds.

00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:44.630
If you’re looking to invest in bonds on
any existing platform whether it be Fidelity,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:50.761
Schwab, or TD Ameritrade, you’ll probably
be greeted with a table that looks like this.

00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:55.031
You have an assortment of maturity dates and
bond types but you don’t really get any

00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:59.790
information about who the issuers are, what
other bonds they offer, or any bond specific

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.261
characteristics.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:05.031
And if you click one of these table entries,
you’re taken to a page like this where you

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:10.210
just see a massive list of every single bond
on the market that fits the given category.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.641
I would imagine that this is rather overwhelming
for someone who is trying to invest in bonds

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:16.331
for the first time.

00:01:16.331 --> 00:01:22.420
And that’s why we wanted to build an experience
that prioritizes the ease of finding and investing

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.141
in the issuers you know and trust.

00:01:25.141 --> 00:01:29.060
For example, let’s say I wanted to invest
in an Apple bond.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:34.551
I can easily navigate to the search tab, look
up Apple, and get a list of all Apple bonds

00:01:34.551 --> 00:01:36.211
sorted by maturity.

00:01:36.211 --> 00:01:40.971
You can also sort by yield, coupon rate, or
our custom suitability score.

00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:45.170
For every bond in the app, we use your investment
goals and profile to create a personalized

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.841
suitability score.

00:01:46.841 --> 00:01:52.310
This Apple bond for example has a suitability
score of 89 and a yield of 5.317%.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.840
You can use this slider to estimate how much
you can expect to earn on a daily, monthly,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.131
and annual basis at the current yield.

00:02:00.131 --> 00:02:04.141
And if you scroll down, you can see all of
the traits of the given bond.

00:02:04.141 --> 00:02:08.141
Speaking of bond characteristics, if you don’t
have a specific issuer in mind, you can navigate

00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:10.051
over to the explore page.

00:02:10.051 --> 00:02:14.691
By default, the explore page pulls up 5 bonds
the best match your investment profile but

00:02:14.691 --> 00:02:16.801
you can further refine the search.

00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:21.091
For example, let’s say, I’m looking for
a corporate investment grade bond that’s

00:02:21.091 --> 00:02:22.301
yielding at least 7%.

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:26.120
I can just enter in these requirements, press
filter, and get a list of bonds that meet

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.601
my requirements sorted by suitability.

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:31.771
Let’s look for an issuer that we recognize.

00:02:31.771 --> 00:02:33.641
Ah here’s one, Paramount Global.

00:02:33.641 --> 00:02:36.370
Let’s say I wanna invest $10,000 into Paramount.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:41.051
I can see that I would earn a little less
than $800 per year.

00:02:41.051 --> 00:02:47.131
If I’m feeling good about this, I can go
ahead and and press earn.

00:02:47.131 --> 00:02:49.911
From here, I can adjust any of the order settings
that I want.

00:02:49.911 --> 00:02:54.661
For example, if I want the order to fill as
quickly as possible, I can change the order

00:02:54.661 --> 00:02:57.131
type to market and press submit.

00:02:57.131 --> 00:03:04.021
The app asks me to authorize using face id
and there we go, the order is submitted.

00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:08.400
Now, if I navigate to the history tab, I can
see my latest order.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:16.021
It looks like it’s already been filled and
if I go over to my portfolio I can see it

00:03:16.021 --> 00:03:20.540
as a new position under Paramount Global.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.801
If I wanna sell, I can do the exact same thing.

00:03:23.801 --> 00:03:28.752
Just press sell, change the order type to
market, and press submit.

00:03:28.752 --> 00:03:30.501
And boom, my order is submitted.

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:36.760
I can go view my order status on history and
it looks like it’s already been filled,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.393
so if I go back and refresh my position, we
can see that it’s now empty.

00:03:41.393 --> 00:03:42.981
And that’s how easy it is to find a bond,
buy it, and sell on Silo.

00:03:42.981 --> 00:03:46.561
Lastly, you have your profile page where you
have general house keeping stuff like statements,

00:03:46.561 --> 00:03:49.001
settings, profile, and things of that nature.

00:03:49.001 --> 00:03:53.771
So, that was kind of a general overview of
Silo, and I hope you would agree that it’s

00:03:53.771 --> 00:03:58.891
far friendlier to use than what’s already
out there but that’s not why we created

00:03:58.891 --> 00:03:59.891
Silo.

00:03:59.891 --> 00:04:01.731
Let me explain.

00:04:01.731 --> 00:04:08.921
WHY SILO:
The main reason that we started developing

00:04:08.921 --> 00:04:15.220
Silo is because an entire generation of investors
are being left behind: Millennials.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:21.071
In fact, on average Millennials tend to hold
65% of their assets in cash, the highest of

00:04:21.071 --> 00:04:22.901
any generation.

00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:28.470
It’s easy to write this off as irresponsible
Millennials who are too busy ordering DoorDash

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.181
instead of focusing on investing and their
financial future, but there’s actually a

00:04:33.181 --> 00:04:37.641
much darker factor at play: the 2008 financial
crisis.

00:04:37.641 --> 00:04:41.011
The 2008 financial crisis affected everyone.

00:04:41.011 --> 00:04:45.291
Millions of people lost their jobs, their
homes, and everything they ever worked for

00:04:45.291 --> 00:04:50.501
while big banks and irresponsible corporations
got to walk away with massive bailouts.

00:04:50.501 --> 00:04:58.101
Wells Fargo: $25 billion, JP Morgan Chase:
$25 billion, Citi Group: another $25 billion,

00:04:58.101 --> 00:04:59.591
and those are just the banks.

00:04:59.591 --> 00:05:06.911
GE walked away with a $140 billion bailout
and AIG walked away with a $182 billion bailout.

00:05:06.911 --> 00:05:11.631
Once again, the real culprits had walked away
scott free while it was the everyday American

00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:12.881
that was left behind.

00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:18.231
But, despite how painful this was, most Americans
did work through this.

00:05:18.231 --> 00:05:23.791
They eventually go new jobs, new houses, and
built up new life savings, everyone except

00:05:23.791 --> 00:05:25.631
for Millennials.

00:05:25.631 --> 00:05:29.421
Ironically, Millennials had lost the least
amount of money given that they were all young

00:05:29.421 --> 00:05:31.691
adults with no money to lose.

00:05:31.691 --> 00:05:36.361
But while they didn’t lose money, they had
lost something much more deeper: a part of

00:05:36.361 --> 00:05:37.881
the American dream.

00:05:37.881 --> 00:05:41.631
They had seen their parents and people around
them lose everything and it gave them a rather

00:05:41.631 --> 00:05:43.711
cynical view of capitalism.

00:05:43.711 --> 00:05:49.031
They felt that there was no reason to be loyal
to a company, no reason to idolize rich people,

00:05:49.031 --> 00:05:53.831
and definitely no reason to invest given that
Wall Street would just stack the cards against

00:05:53.831 --> 00:05:54.831
them.

00:05:54.831 --> 00:05:57.871
15 years later, these fears are still very
much alive.

00:05:57.871 --> 00:06:03.150
In fact, a whopping 80% of Millennials admit
that the crisis still influences their investing

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.921
to this day.

00:06:04.921 --> 00:06:08.211
And this is largely why they tend to hoard
cash.

00:06:08.211 --> 00:06:13.720
In fact, nearly 1 in 4 millennials have savings
of $100,000 or more, yet stock market participation

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:17.590
is still lower than it was 23 years ago.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.071
Several new age investing companies have tried
to address this.

00:06:21.071 --> 00:06:25.530
On the stock side of things, you’ve got
people like Robinhood, Webull, Public.com,

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.911
MooMoo, and M1 Finance.

00:06:27.911 --> 00:06:32.970
And on the crypto side of things, you’ve
got people like Coinbase, Binance, and a lot

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:33.970
more till recently.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:39.511
But, while these guys have increased market
participation, I don’t think they’ve necessarily

00:06:39.511 --> 00:06:44.911
increased investing participation given that
these guys aren’t exactly encouraging people

00:06:44.911 --> 00:06:45.911
to invest.

00:06:45.911 --> 00:06:51.131
Rather, they’re encouraging people to trade
stocks and options or 10X their money with

00:06:51.131 --> 00:06:53.071
crypto.

00:06:53.071 --> 00:06:59.060
Much of their pitch is trying to make investing
fun and exciting but the reality is that investing

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:01.071
should not be exhilarating.

00:07:01.071 --> 00:07:04.840
If it is, you’re probably gambling, not
investing.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:08.711
Of course, you’ll always hear about some
random guy who made millions by betting on

00:07:08.711 --> 00:07:14.301
some obscure stock or crypto, but chances
are, you probably won't be turning a $1000

00:07:14.301 --> 00:07:15.891
into a million by trading.

00:07:15.891 --> 00:07:19.931
You’re actually more likely to lose the
initial $1000.

00:07:19.931 --> 00:07:22.470
Why do platforms sell this vision then you
ask?

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:23.870
Well, two reasons.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:28.650
For one, untold riches is the easiest way
to get people to open accounts and deposit

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.650
money.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:33.741
And two, traders generate far more commissions
than buy and hold investors.

00:07:33.741 --> 00:07:39.671
And as such, we’ve seen this massive gamification
of the entire investing space.

00:07:39.671 --> 00:07:43.801
Real investing, however, isn’t about making
millions overnight.

00:07:43.801 --> 00:07:47.930
Real investing is about making responsible
and calculated moves to grow wealth over an

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:54.780
entire lifetime, and this is what we would
like to bring to market with Silo.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:08:02.791
WHAT ARE BONDS:
Long term, we would like to offer much more

00:08:02.791 --> 00:08:08.440
than just bonds but we decided to start with
bonds because its by far the most underappreciated

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:13.121
and underserved asset class especially when
it comes to everyday investors.

00:08:13.121 --> 00:08:17.460
You see, all of the biggest companies and
investors in the world own hundreds of billions

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.141
of dollars worth of bonds.

00:08:19.141 --> 00:08:20.361
Take Apple for instance.

00:08:20.361 --> 00:08:24.851
They’re currently holding $34 billion in
short term marketable securities and $104

00:08:24.851 --> 00:08:29.551
billion in long term marketable securities
most of which is bills and bonds.

00:08:29.551 --> 00:08:35.171
Similarly, Microsoft is holding $144 billion
in cash, cash equivalents, and short term

00:08:35.171 --> 00:08:36.171
investments.

00:08:36.171 --> 00:08:40.151
Even Warren Buffett, the legendary stock investor,
is playing with a hundred billion worth of

00:08:40.151 --> 00:08:41.640
treasury bills.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.491
All of this is only a mere fraction of the
entire bond market though.

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:51.251
The global bond market is worth $133 trillion
which is even larger than the global stock

00:08:51.251 --> 00:08:55.631
market which is “only” worth $109 trillion.

00:08:55.631 --> 00:09:01.611
But despite the raw size and ubiquity of bonds,
the everyday investor has only heard about

00:09:01.611 --> 00:09:03.501
bonds in passing.

00:09:03.501 --> 00:09:06.301
So what even are Bonds?

00:09:06.301 --> 00:09:11.031
Well, before we get into all the technical
details, let’s take a step back and understand

00:09:11.031 --> 00:09:13.471
the role you play in the bond market.

00:09:13.471 --> 00:09:18.341
As an individual, you get the unique opportunity
to help large companies and our government

00:09:18.341 --> 00:09:19.471
raise money.

00:09:19.471 --> 00:09:24.131
These institutions really only have two main
ways they can raise money: sell parts of their

00:09:24.131 --> 00:09:26.411
company or get a loan.

00:09:26.411 --> 00:09:29.661
When they sell a part of the company, you’re
able to buy a piece of it through the stock

00:09:29.661 --> 00:09:30.661
market.

00:09:30.661 --> 00:09:34.820
Now, if you’re the average millennial, you’re
probably not the biggest fan of stock investing

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.080
because you saw what happened in 2008.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.551
Any company could very well crash and burn,
rendering their stock completely useless.

00:09:42.551 --> 00:09:46.471
After all, didn’t Amazon themselves lose
money for a decade?

00:09:46.471 --> 00:09:51.191
- this all sounds a bit too risky, and you’re
not wrong.

00:09:51.191 --> 00:09:56.721
Investing in individual stocks can not only
be risky but unpredictable, but this risk

00:09:56.721 --> 00:10:00.071
also comes along with a substantial amount
upside potential.

00:10:00.071 --> 00:10:05.091
If you’re just looking for a stable reliable
investment that pays out a set amount though,

00:10:05.091 --> 00:10:07.890
this may be where bonds could be of value.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.820
Instead of buying a piece of the company,
with bonds, you lend to the company under

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.091
a legally binding contract.

00:10:14.091 --> 00:10:17.510
And if they don’t pay you back, a bunch
of lawyers would knock down their door and

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.901
sell everything they have left to pay you
back as much as possible.

00:10:20.901 --> 00:10:24.721
When a company agrees to this arrangement,
they agreeing to raise debt, otherwise known

00:10:24.721 --> 00:10:26.811
as issuing bonds.

00:10:26.811 --> 00:10:31.741
And these very bonds are available for us
to purchase; however, they work a bit differently

00:10:31.741 --> 00:10:32.741
than stocks.

00:10:32.741 --> 00:10:37.820
For starters, the most basic characteristic
of a bond is it’s “face value”.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.121
This is usually $1000 and it’s the amount
of money that the company is promising to

00:10:42.121 --> 00:10:43.591
you pay you back.

00:10:43.591 --> 00:10:47.591
This doesn’t mean that you’ll always buy
a bond at face value though.

00:10:47.591 --> 00:10:51.841
Oftentimes, the market price of a bond will
be lower than its face value.

00:10:51.841 --> 00:10:57.021
For example, an Apple bond with a face value
of $1000 may be trading at $950.

00:10:57.021 --> 00:11:01.140
This means that you only have to pay $950
to purchase this bond but Apple will pay you

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.651
back $1000 when the bond matures.

00:11:03.651 --> 00:11:07.950
If the bond’s maturity is 1 year out, you’ll
earn a profit of $50 at the end of the year

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:10.681
which translates to roughly a 5% yield.

00:11:10.681 --> 00:11:16.371
In the meantime, most bonds will also pay
out interest payments known as “coupon payments”.

00:11:16.371 --> 00:11:21.271
A coupon payment is a percentage of the face
value that gets paid out usually twice a year.

00:11:21.271 --> 00:11:27.151
For example, let’s say that same Apple bond
had a coupon rate of 2%, well then, on top

00:11:27.151 --> 00:11:32.080
of $50 that you already earned, you would
earn another $20 in the form of coupon payments.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:36.390
Aka, your total profit from the bond would
be $70 meaning that your total yield would

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.181
be roughly 7%.

00:11:38.181 --> 00:11:45.181
Of course, you could really get into the weeds
of but in a general sense, that’s about

00:11:45.181 --> 00:11:46.181
it.

00:11:46.181 --> 00:11:49.271
You lend a company a bit of your money, they
pay you some interest for it, and once the

00:11:49.271 --> 00:11:54.801
contract is over, they pay you back and go
about their business, but how safe really

00:11:54.801 --> 00:11:56.700
is all of this?

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:06.390
COMMITMENT TO SECURITY:
One key features of bonds is their consistent

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:07.631
and predictable interest payments.

00:12:07.631 --> 00:12:11.801
Since there is a legal obligation for the
lender to pay you back, failure to make those

00:12:11.801 --> 00:12:14.831
timely payments puts lenders in default.

00:12:14.831 --> 00:12:19.021
Just like us individuals have credit ratings
that measure how financially responsible we

00:12:19.021 --> 00:12:23.070
are, companies have credit ratings that measure
the likelihood of default.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:28.241
These ratings are split into two categories:
investment-grade and high-yield, or “junk”,

00:12:28.241 --> 00:12:31.561
with investment-grade bonds being seen as
the safer of the two.

00:12:31.561 --> 00:12:38.081
In fact, in 14 of the past 22 years, investment-grade
corporate bonds did not default AT ALL.

00:12:38.081 --> 00:12:42.871
Also, about “junk” bonds, these definitely
have a scary name we wouldn’t personally

00:12:42.871 --> 00:12:48.921
recommend these, but all things considered,
they’re actually rather safe as well.

00:12:48.921 --> 00:12:53.890
Even with all the talk about inflation and
interest rates for example, as of June, the

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:59.781
high-yield bond default rate was still only
1.9% which just goes to show how seriously

00:12:59.781 --> 00:13:01.411
companies take paying back bondholders.

00:13:01.411 --> 00:13:07.231
On Silo, there’s over 2,000 corporate issuers
that you can choose from but if you’re really

00:13:07.231 --> 00:13:12.211
concerned about minimizing risk, your best
bet would likely be government bills and bonds.

00:13:12.211 --> 00:13:17.411
The federal government’s bonds, aka treasuries,
work the same way as any other bond.

00:13:17.411 --> 00:13:22.221
However, because the government issues them,
they are seen as some of the safest investments

00:13:22.221 --> 00:13:23.221
available.

00:13:23.221 --> 00:13:29.101
Now, let me preface by saying that there is
no such thing as a risk free investment.

00:13:29.101 --> 00:13:33.341
All investments carry risk, that’s kind
of why they generate a return in the first

00:13:33.341 --> 00:13:34.341
place.

00:13:34.341 --> 00:13:38.450
But, in the spectrum of risk, US government
bonds are as close as you’re gonna get when

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.621
it comes to minimizing risk.

00:13:40.621 --> 00:13:45.091
In fact, the only time that a financial institution
is allowed to describe an investment as risk

00:13:45.091 --> 00:13:48.471
free is when they’re referring to treasury
yields.

00:13:48.471 --> 00:13:52.271
Treasury yields are considered “risk free”
because they’re backed by the US federal

00:13:52.271 --> 00:13:56.981
government who in theory can just print money
to pay back bond holders.

00:13:56.981 --> 00:14:01.940
When it comes to safety and security though,
the investment itself is only half the story.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:06.880
The platform through which you invest is also
equally important and that’s why we’re

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:14.581
proud to say that each Silo account is protected
by $500,000 in SIPC insurance and $2.5 million

00:14:14.581 --> 00:14:15.781
in FDIC insurance.

00:14:15.781 --> 00:14:19.801
Also, all funds deposited on the platform
will be maintained by our trusted custodian,

00:14:19.801 --> 00:14:25.251
Interactive Brokers, who has experience managing
$373 billion worth of assets over the past

00:14:25.251 --> 00:14:26.251
45 years.

00:14:26.251 --> 00:14:32.121
So, with Silo, you not only get the sleek
UI and friendly interface of a modern investing

00:14:32.121 --> 00:14:36.961
platform but you also get the experience and
stability of an established brokerage.

00:14:36.961 --> 00:14:41.200
All of that probably sounds nice and all,
but you’re probably wondering, how much

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:45.891
will all of this cost?

00:14:45.891 --> 00:14:52.021
SILO PRICING
When it comes to investing, one key consideration

00:14:52.021 --> 00:14:53.021
is cost.

00:14:53.021 --> 00:14:57.711
When Robinhood entered the market back in
2014, they disrupted the market by cutting

00:14:57.711 --> 00:15:02.621
commissions and fees, which played an important
role in the buzz they generated around trading.

00:15:02.621 --> 00:15:08.521
However, as we found out, Robinhood still
makes money from your trades, just not transparently.

00:15:08.521 --> 00:15:13.080
Instead, they use something called payment
for order flow which is essentially just a

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:16.651
hidden markup that’s built into the price
of the stock.

00:15:16.651 --> 00:15:21.330
This is not only a frowned upon practice,
but a straight up illegal one in most countries

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:22.591
outside the US.

00:15:22.591 --> 00:15:26.880
In fact, the EU just banned this practice
a couple of months ago.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:31.140
The bond market doesn’t use payment for
order flow but bond brokers usually earn money

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.640
in a similar fashion: through markups.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:36.940
For example, let’s say a given bond is trading
at $950.

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:43.151
To buy this bond, you’ll probably actually
have to pay $965 because the average bond

00:15:43.151 --> 00:15:46.330
markup is $15.14.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.591
And this applies when you’re selling as
well.

00:15:48.591 --> 00:15:52.630
Instead of being subject to a markup though,
you’ll be subject to a markdown.

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:59.181
So, if a bond is trading for $950, you may
only be able to sell it for $935.

00:15:59.181 --> 00:16:04.801
In other words, for every bond you buy and
sell, you would be losing $30 to markups and

00:16:04.801 --> 00:16:05.801
markdowns.

00:16:05.801 --> 00:16:10.211
At Silo, we wanted to make sure you don’t
have to worry about any costs while buying

00:16:10.211 --> 00:16:11.211
bonds.

00:16:11.211 --> 00:16:14.591
And that’s why we have absolutely no markups
or markdowns at Silo.

00:16:14.591 --> 00:16:18.981
The execution fees for each order is also
passed on at cost.

00:16:18.981 --> 00:16:21.640
How did we accomplish this, you ask?

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:37.741
Well, simple, by aligning our interests with
yours through a subscription fee.

00:16:37.741 --> 00:16:41.661
Regardless of whether you buy only one bond
per month or are investing tens of thousands

00:16:41.661 --> 00:16:45.401
on a daily basis, the price you see is the
price you pay.

00:16:45.401 --> 00:16:49.870
The only way we make money is through a $10
subscription fee that is applied to accounts

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.671
that are larger than $3000.

00:16:52.671 --> 00:16:57.721
As long as you buy at least 8 bonds per year,
the monthly fee will pay for itself.

00:16:57.721 --> 00:17:02.691
Also, if you deposit less than $3000, the
platform is truly free to use.

00:17:02.691 --> 00:17:06.510
Whether it's the bond prices themselves or
the execution fees, everything is passed on

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.610
at cost, we don’t make a dime.

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:13.620
Moreover, unlike other fintech platforms,
we’re not incentivized to get you to trade

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:17.401
because we make the same amount regardless
of how much you trade.

00:17:17.401 --> 00:17:21.470
So, our goals are aligned: to make sure that
you make investing decisions that benefit

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:23.110
you over the long term.

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:27.201
With that being said, we would also like to
be honest about who this platform is actually

00:17:27.201 --> 00:17:30.281
for because it’s not for everyone.

00:17:30.281 --> 00:17:39.441
WHO SILO IS FOR:
While we’d love for as many of y’all to

00:17:39.441 --> 00:17:45.491
use the platform, we think there is a certain
group of people who would benefit most from

00:17:45.491 --> 00:17:52.531
Silo, while others would benefit more from
other investments, whether it be stocks, real

00:17:52.531 --> 00:17:55.241
estate, indexes, or simply holding cash in
a savings account.

00:17:55.241 --> 00:17:57.831
In today’s current rate environment, high
yield savings accounts are yielding rates

00:17:57.831 --> 00:18:01.991
up to 5%, meaning that holding cash in a high-yield
savings account is a good option.

00:18:01.991 --> 00:18:07.671
But what about cash that you have in a Chase
or Wells Fargo account?

00:18:07.671 --> 00:18:12.220
Big banks are notorious for shilling out fractional
interest rates on your cash.

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:15.791
If you’re someone that’s holding cash
that isn’t benefiting from the high interest

00:18:15.791 --> 00:18:20.701
rate environment or worse, getting eaten up
by high inflation, a good alternative is holding

00:18:20.701 --> 00:18:24.331
short-term treasuries that provide rates as
high as 5.5%.

00:18:24.331 --> 00:18:30.110
Also, if you’ve stockpiled a bunch of cash
into a high-yield savings account, one downside

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:34.191
you should consider is that high interest
rates savings accounts only last as long as

00:18:34.191 --> 00:18:36.340
the fed keeps interest rates high.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:40.611
In one, two, or a few years from now, decreases
in interest rates will send high-yield savings

00:18:40.611 --> 00:18:42.051
rates back to the bottom.

00:18:42.051 --> 00:18:46.531
So, if you don’t wanna play that game and
just wanna lock-in a rate for the next decade

00:18:46.531 --> 00:18:52.291
or further, 10 or 30 year treasuries or even
long-term corporate bonds may make sense.

00:18:52.291 --> 00:18:56.101
Ironically, this strategy may apply to the
other side as well.

00:18:56.101 --> 00:19:00.191
For example, let's say you’re heavily invested
in stocks and are seeing that your returns

00:19:00.191 --> 00:19:04.331
over the past year haven’t been that great
or want to reduce volatility.

00:19:04.331 --> 00:19:07.531
Well then, it may be time to diversify.

00:19:07.531 --> 00:19:11.281
Investing in bonds at these rates means earning
much more consistent and secure income that

00:19:11.281 --> 00:19:14.360
has even matched stock market performance
over the past year.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:16.610
Also, what about real estate?

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:21.821
With mortgage rates at levels past 8%, it
may make more sense to earn 5% through bonds

00:19:21.821 --> 00:19:24.231
than pay 8% on a property.

00:19:24.231 --> 00:19:28.691
Now a lot of these have to do with the condition
our economy is currently in.

00:19:28.691 --> 00:19:33.271
One type of investor that may benefit from
bond investing regardless of current conditions

00:19:33.271 --> 00:19:37.671
is a more conservative investor who has some
level of apprehension towards stocks.

00:19:37.671 --> 00:19:42.860
Maybe, you see stocks as too risky, unpredictable,
and volatile but also feel that you should

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:44.831
be doing some sort of investing.

00:19:44.831 --> 00:19:47.961
Well then, bonds could be the perfect middle
ground for you.

00:19:47.961 --> 00:19:53.771
Sure, they wont have the same return potential
as stocks but it may be a more comfortable

00:19:53.771 --> 00:19:54.771
investment.

00:19:54.771 --> 00:19:59.851
As you can see, bonds can be a valuable asset
to a wide range of investors but to be frank

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:03.791
there’s a lot of people for whom bonds are
not the right choice.

00:20:03.791 --> 00:20:13.871
WHO SILO IS NOT FOR:
Likely the most obvious group of people for

00:20:13.871 --> 00:20:19.861
whom Silo would not be a good fit for is if
you’re not able to invest at least $5,000

00:20:19.861 --> 00:20:22.751
or ideally at least $10,000.

00:20:22.751 --> 00:20:27.501
For one, the monthly fee would eat into a
lot of your returns but even putting the fee

00:20:27.501 --> 00:20:30.731
aside, bonds in general probably aren’t
a great fit for you.

00:20:30.731 --> 00:20:33.960
For example, let’s say you invest $1000.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:39.201
Even if you lock in a 8% yield, you’re only
earning $80 per year which brings up the question

00:20:39.201 --> 00:20:40.921
of whether that’s even worthwhile.

00:20:40.921 --> 00:20:45.381
It’s only when you get into the tens of
thousands that bond yields really start to

00:20:45.381 --> 00:20:46.381
add up.

00:20:46.381 --> 00:20:51.161
Also, if you’re not in a position where
you can invest $5 or 10,000, it would probably

00:20:51.161 --> 00:20:55.801
be a better idea to build up larger cash reserves
and an emergency fund before investing in

00:20:55.801 --> 00:20:56.801
anything.

00:20:56.801 --> 00:21:00.991
Someone else that bonds may not be a good
fit for is someone who already has a solid

00:21:00.991 --> 00:21:04.970
investing plan in place, and investing in
bonds would take away from that.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:08.970
For example, maybe you already invest everything
you’re comfortable with investing into the

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.891
S&amp;P 500 every month.

00:21:10.891 --> 00:21:15.230
You should not start investing in bonds if
it would take away from this existing plan.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:20.071
In other words, bond investing should be something
that you add to your investing strategy, not

00:21:20.071 --> 00:21:23.271
something that replaces your existing strategy.

00:21:23.271 --> 00:21:26.631
Bonds would also not be a good fit for you
if you have a high risk tolerance and are

00:21:26.631 --> 00:21:28.511
looking for extraordinary returns.

00:21:28.511 --> 00:21:33.001
The truth is that even if you’re investing
the highest yielding junk bonds, you won’t

00:21:33.001 --> 00:21:37.850
be generating 5 or 10X returns, so if that’s
what you’re looking for and you’re willing

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:42.431
to take the risk, stocks and crypto would
probably be a better option.

00:21:42.431 --> 00:21:47.261
Speaking of returns, since bonds pay a fixed
interest rate, there really is no compounding

00:21:47.261 --> 00:21:48.261
effect.

00:21:48.261 --> 00:21:52.011
When you try to reinvest your coupon payments,
there is no guarantee that bond yields will

00:21:52.011 --> 00:21:54.671
be at the same level as when you originally
invested.

00:21:54.671 --> 00:21:58.101
For example, a 30-year treasury bond today
yields around 5%.

00:21:58.101 --> 00:22:03.621
But 10 years down the line, if 30 year treasuries
are only yielding 3%, you wouldn’t be able

00:22:03.621 --> 00:22:06.631
to reinvest the interest payments at the same
5% yield.

00:22:06.631 --> 00:22:12.191
So, if you want a single avenue to invest
into and see your cash compound, index funds,

00:22:12.191 --> 00:22:15.481
ETFs, and mutual funds may make more sense.

00:22:15.481 --> 00:22:20.011
Silo would probably also not be a good fit
for you if you have cash but can’t afford

00:22:20.011 --> 00:22:22.460
to lock it up for a set period of time.

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:26.711
Aka, if you have a high need for liquidity,
investing in bonds is not the right choice

00:22:26.711 --> 00:22:27.711
for you.

00:22:27.711 --> 00:22:31.831
Rather, it would make more sense to keep your
money in a high-yield savings account without

00:22:31.831 --> 00:22:35.781
a lock in period so that you can take it out
whenever you need.

00:22:35.781 --> 00:22:39.961
Honestly, this is starting to sound like one
of those disclaimers at the end of a medicine

00:22:39.961 --> 00:22:48.011
commercial, so let’s move on.

00:22:48.011 --> 00:22:51.811
SILO:
At the end of the day, high inflation and

00:22:51.811 --> 00:22:56.871
high interest rates have led us into an environment
that we haven’t seen since really the 1970s.

00:22:56.871 --> 00:23:02.081
As such, the value proposition of every asset
class has drastically changed and the asset

00:23:02.081 --> 00:23:05.391
class the likely benefited the most is fixed
income.

00:23:05.391 --> 00:23:10.791
Of course we’re biased, but we believe that
we have created a stellar bond investing platform

00:23:10.791 --> 00:23:14.330
that redefines how retail investors are able
to invest in bonds.

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:20.401
By choosing Silo, you’re choosing transparency,
ease of use, and most importantly, a wide

00:23:20.401 --> 00:23:23.341
selection of secure and consistent investments.

00:23:23.341 --> 00:23:27.230
So, if you’re in the market for something
like that, please consider checking out Silo

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:29.031
